const glossary=[
 ['Atom','A neutral particle with a nucleus and electrons. In this explorer, one trapped atom carries one physical qubit in two chosen internal states.'],
 ['MOT · magneto-optical trap','Laser beams plus a magnetic-field gradient slow atoms and push them toward a central region. This produces a cold cloud, not a grid of individual qubits.'],
 ['Optical tweezer','A tightly focused laser beam creates a dipole-force trap. The light holds the atom’s position; a separate control interaction changes its qubit state.'],
 ['Reservoir','A stock of cold atoms available to load or replenish traps. It supplies atoms; it does not store the computational state. Some machines load directly without a separate reservoir.'],
 ['Cooling','Reducing the random motion of atoms. Laser cooling exports energy through scattered light. Cooling and qubit-state initialization are separate operations.'],
 ['Hyperfine state','An internal atomic energy level arising from coupling between electronic and nuclear angular momentum. Two Rb hyperfine levels can encode a qubit.'],
 ['Nuclear spin','An intrinsic quantum property of the nucleus. The two projections of nuclear spin ½ in ¹⁷¹Yb can serve as |0⟩ and |1⟩.'],
 ['Superposition','A coherent combination of |0⟩ and |1⟩ with amplitudes and a relative phase. Measurement probabilities depend on the measurement basis.'],
 ['Coherence','The preservation of relative phase relationships. Noise can spoil interference even when a measurement in the Z basis still gives 50/50 results.'],
 ['Rabi pulse','A calibrated coherent drive. Pulse area sets a rotation angle; frequency and detuning select the transition, and phase selects the rotation direction.'],
 ['Rydberg state','A highly excited electronic state. Its strong interactions with nearby Rydberg atoms can mediate entangling gates. It is usually a temporary gate resource.'],
 ['Blockade','The interaction shifts the energy of simultaneous Rydberg excitation. If that shift exceeds the drive scale, a second resonant excitation is suppressed.'],
 ['Entanglement','A joint state whose quantum description cannot be split into independent states for each qubit. A two-qubit gate can create it from suitable inputs.'],
 ['CZ / CNOT','CZ changes the sign of |11⟩. CNOT flips a target bit when its control is 1. Hadamard gates on the target before and after CZ convert it to CNOT.'],
 ['Physical qubit','A hardware carrier and its two-state quantum degree of freedom. Here this is one atom and two selected internal states.'],
 ['Logical qubit','Quantum information encoded across a code block of physical qubits. Checks reveal error information without directly reading the encoded amplitudes.'],
 ['Syndrome','The outcomes of error checks. A decoder uses them to infer likely errors. It does not receive a copy of the unknown quantum state.'],
 ['Code distance','The minimum weight of a nontrivial logical operator in a code. A distance-d code can correct up to floor((d−1)/2) arbitrary single-qubit errors under ideal conditions.'],
 ['AOD / SLM','An acousto-optic deflector steers beams using sound waves; a spatial light modulator shapes wavefronts. These devices form or rearrange tweezer arrays.']
];
const headings={supply:['01 / ATOM SUPPLY','From atom source to qubit array.','Source, cooling, trapping, and array assembly.'],control:['02 / SINGLE-QUBIT CONTROL','Control a two-state qubit.','Adjust the amplitudes and relative phase.'],gates:['03 / TWO-QUBIT GATES','Two-qubit interactions and gates.','A temporary Rydberg excitation enables an interaction.'],readout:['04 / MEASUREMENT','Measure a quantum state.','Choose a basis, repeat the experiment, count the outcomes.'],logical:['05 / PHYSICAL → LOGICAL','Encoding and error correction.','Encode information across physical qubits and check for errors.'],sources:['REFERENCE DESK','Sources and model assumptions.','Experimental references and model limits.']};
